{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: when the last 10–15 days show a strong uptrend (higher highs / higher lows), add an upward level shift to the whole path (≈ +2 to +6 €/t), with the largest uplift typically needed in days 5–20.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model underestimates slope during sharp rallies (it smooths and reacts late). Increase day-to-day increments for the first ~2 weeks when recent momentum is positive; don’t rely on quick mean reversion.",
    "Premature peak + early mean reversion: forecasts often top out too early and start drifting down while realized prices keep rising. Delay any projected rollover unless the last ~5–7 realized sessions show clear loss of momentum.",
    "Downturn overshoot after highs: once the market finally turns, the model’s decline is too shallow (it stays too high vs realized late in the horizon). If a reversal is confirmed (e.g., 3–5 consecutive lower closes), steepen the projected drawdown for days ~20–30.",
    "Horizon shape bias: mid-horizon (≈ days 7–20) is systematically too flat/low in upswings; late horizon (≈ days 25–30) tends to be too high if a reversal occurs. Apply a hump-shaped correction: uplift mid-horizon in rallies, but allow a sharper tail-off if reversal signals appear."
  ]
}
